# CLI tools (device-agnostic).
#
# The modern replacements Deboma installs (omarchy's "CLI / tooling" section),
# plus the small utilities its aliases and helper scripts expect. Nothing here
# is a service or a daemon: every line is a program that runs when it is invoked
# and exits, so nothing stays resident in the 4 GB of this machine.
#
# packages/base.list keeps what the *system* needs; this file is what the *user*
# reaches for in a terminal. Every package is wired up by
# uis/sway/files/etc/profile.d/90-cli-tools.sh (environment) and
# uis/sway/files/home/.bashrc (aliases, prompt, key bindings); the Arch -> Debian
# renames and the deliberate omissions are documented in AGENTS.md.

# --- the replacements -------------------------------------------------------
# ls. Debian's eza is the maintainer's fork-aware original; the aliases in
# ~/.bashrc are Deboma's (ls, lsa, lt, lta).
eza
# cat and the manual pager. Debian installs the binary as **batcat** (the name
# `bat` belongs to another package), so rootfs-setup.sh adds the
# /usr/local/bin/bat symlink the aliases and MANPAGER call.
bat
# find. Debian's binary is **fdfind** for the same reason; rootfs-setup.sh adds
# /usr/local/bin/fd, which is also what FZF_DEFAULT_COMMAND uses.
fd-find
# grep, as rg. Nothing aliases `grep`: rg is not a drop-in for every flag, and
# a silently different grep in an interactive shell is a trap.  rg is also what
# "Search the manual" in rofi-learn uses.
ripgrep
# Fuzzy finder, for the Ctrl-R/Ctrl-T/Alt-C bindings (~/.bashrc sources
# /usr/share/fzf/key-bindings.bash), the `ff` preview picker, and the terminal
# file search lazyos-find (Alt+Search), which is why it is a hard dependency of a
# key rather than only of the shell.
fzf
# cd, by frecency (`z`, `zi`); ~/.bashrc wraps `cd` so real paths still work.
zoxide
# The prompt. Deboma's starship.toml is shipped at ~/.config/starship.toml,
# which is why the shell is not Debian's plain one.
starship
# tldr, with the pages cached locally (the binary is `tldr`).
tealdeer
# top.
btop
# fastfetch, the system summary Deboma shows on demand. Not wired into the
# login shell: the bar already reports the session, and a fetch on every
# terminal would add a process to every window.
fastfetch
# inxi, for the hardware questions check-system.sh does not answer (audio
# codecs, USB, PCI).
inxi

# --- terminal multiplexer / TUIs --------------------------------------------
tmux
# git, the TUI. Nothing calls it automatically (`g` is still git).
lazygit
# gum: the shell-script TUI toolkit Deboma's helpers are written with; this
# port's bar scripts do not need it, but writing a prompt/menu in a script does.
gum

# --- small utilities --------------------------------------------------------
# curl: base.list does not include it and several tools (yt-dlp, tldr's update,
# npm) expect it.
curl
unzip
# zip: unzip ships alone, so creating an archive is the half that was missing.
zip
whois
# strace: the tool a device with no debugger reaches for, and the one that
# answers "which file is it opening" without a rebuild.
strace
# ncdu: the disk usage walk a 58 GB eMMC needs when it fills up; `du` prints the
# same numbers as a wall of text.
ncdu
# socat and inotify-tools (inotifywait): the two that make "watch this until
# that happens" a one-liner.
socat
inotify-tools
# qrencode / zbar-tools: generate and read QR codes from the terminal.
qrencode
zbar-tools
# OCR, used by hand on screenshots. Deboma's screenshot stack calls it from its
# own script; here it is only the tool.
tesseract-ocr
tesseract-ocr-eng

# --- editors ----------------------------------------------------------------
# nano is in packages/base.list: it is the editor that is always there. These
# two are what make setting $EDITOR worth it - ~/.config/bin/eff, git and
# `crontab -e` all open whatever $EDITOR names, and etc/profile.d/90-cli-tools.sh
# now points it here.
#
# neovim: 9.7 MB plus neovim-runtime (27 MB), /usr/bin/nvim. It is Omarchy's
# editor, brings its own LuaJIT and tree-sitter library (which is why the
# standalone tree-sitter-cli and lua5.4 below stay out), starts with no
# configuration, and speaks LSP through plugins the user installs into
# ~/.config/nvim - nothing of that is shipped.
neovim
# vim: 4.3 MB plus vim-runtime (38.8 MB), /usr/bin/vim.basic, and its postinst
# registers the `vi` alternative (priority 30) - so /usr/bin/vi exists without
# vim-tiny, the package this would otherwise need. It is the editor for a
# machine where nvim's runtime is not wanted, and `vi` is what a script written
# for POSIX expects to find.
vim

# --- keys / encryption ------------------------------------------------------
# gpg: the Developer menu (Alt+Ctrl+D) encrypts the profile archive with it -
# symmetric AES256, with the passphrase fed through file descriptor 3 because
# that is what a rofi prompt can hand over (age -p only reads a terminal). It is
# also the binary a developer with a keyring expects: `git commit -S` and
# `gpg --export-secret-keys` are the same client, which is why this is gnupg
# (gpg, gpg-agent, dirmngr, gpgsm, the localizations) and not the bare `gpg`
# package: signing needs the agent, and a gpg without one fails at the first
# signed commit instead of at the moment something depends on it. No unit is
# enabled: the packaged gpg-agent socket starts the agent on demand and it idles
# out by itself (the Developer menu's export starts it too, because gpg 2.x
# routes the passphrase through the agent - check-system.sh lists it among the
# expected user units for that reason).
gnupg

# --- dictation -------------------------------------------------------------
# whisper.cpp: the dictation shortcut records with parecord and transcribes with
# whisper-cli. The package ships the CLI tools only; the model (78 MB for the
# default tiny.en) is fetched on first use by ~/.config/bin/dictate-model and
# verified against a pinned SHA256.
whisper.cpp-tools
# wtype: types the transcription into the focused window. It is also what an
# emoji picker needs to insert its glyph, so it is not dictation-only.
wtype

# --- local model ------------------------------------------------------------
# llama.cpp: the model window (~/.config/bin/lazyos-model, Alt+Ctrl+M) runs a
# GGUF file with `llama-cli`, and `--bench` re-measures this machine with
# `llama-bench`. This is the *Debian package*, not a build: forky's 1:0.7.0
# runtime-dispatches to libggml-cpu-armv8.2 (the dotprod kernels this SoC has),
# a platform build with `-DGGML_CPU_ARM_ARCH=armv8.2-a+dotprod+fp16` measured
# +1.7% (11.14 against 10.95 tok/s) for 396 s of build, so tools/llama.cpp/ is
# not allowed to exist. The weights are not in the image either (2.4 GB): a
# person fetches them with `lazyos-model --fetch`, verified against the pinned
# SHA256 in ~/.config/lazyos/models.sha256, the way dictate-model fetches
# whisper's. The LLM itself is the tool here, and it only runs while its window
# is open.
llama.cpp-examples

# --- media ------------------------------------------------------------------
yt-dlp
# ffmpeg is what yt-dlp uses to merge a video stream and an audio stream; without
# it yt-dlp can only save single-file formats. It is also the transcoder the
# terminal instructions assume exists.
ffmpeg

# --- manuals ----------------------------------------------------------------
# man itself: the image ships `--help` and nothing else without it, and the
# MANPAGER below only exists to make it pretty.
man-db
# The manual pages themselves, rendered: glow (a markdown reader) is what
# rofi-learn opens them with, and its own style is ~/.config/glow/solarized.json.
# It is a single binary with no runtime, and it only runs while a page is on
# screen.
glow
# col(1), which MANPAGER pipes through to strip overstrike; it comes from the
# util-linux extras, not from a package named "col".
bsdextrautils

# --- JavaScript runtime -----------------------------------------------------
# Deboma installs these only for the third-party agent CLIs (Claude Code,
# Codex, Grok), which upstream gets through mise; Debian has no mise, and
# `npm install -g` is the route that works on both. They are tools, not
# services, and are unused by the session itself.
nodejs
npm

# --- deliberately not here --------------------------------------------------
# Deboma installs these, and this port does not, because each of them is either a
# background job or only meaningful to a program this port does not have:
#
#   plocate        `locate` plus plocate-updatedb.timer, a systemd timer that
#                  indexes the whole disk. A timer is still a service (it runs
#                  without anyone asking), and 4 GB of RAM with a card that is
#                  rewritten on every install is not where it pays off.
#   tree-sitter-cli, lua5.4
#                  editor and Keybindings-view tooling for Omarchy's own
#                  components; nothing in this session parses a grammar or Lua.
#   gawk           installed upstream because omarchy's theme renderer needs
#                  awk's RT variable; the solarized theme here is a static file,
#                  and Debian's mawk is the faster default.
#   libnotify-bin  notify-send is already pulled in by grimshot (or by mako's
#                  client tools), so listing it would change nothing.
#   jq, wl-clipboard
#                  already in uis/sway/packages.list (the bar and the session
#                  use them).
